<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="FCO3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Foundational Concepts and Objectives</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-P3">
    <title>Purpose</title>
      <p> 
        In this activity, you will investigate the structure and dynamics of the hydrosphere—Earth’s interconnected water systems. 
        Through a series of modeling exercises, you will:
        </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Represent the distribution of water across various reservoirs, including oceans, glaciers, surface water, and groundwater.       
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Examine how oceanic circulation redistributes heat and influences global weather and climate patterns.        
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Analyze the processes of groundwater recharge and depletion and consider ecological and human impacts on aquifer systems.        
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        <p>
            This exercise emphasizes systems thinking and ecological interactions, helping you understand how water shapes and sustains life on Earth.
        </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-obj3">
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
      <p> You will: </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 2.1:</term> Describe the components and structure of the hydrosphere, including the distribution of water in oceans, glaciers, groundwater, surface water, and the atmosphere.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 2.2:</term> Explain the mechanisms driving the water cycle, including evaporation, condensation, precipitation, infiltration, and runoff, and how these processes contribute to the movement of water through Earth's systems.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 2.3:</term> Differentiate between surface and deepwater ocean currents, identifying the physical forces that drive each and their roles in global heat transfer, nutrient cycling, and climate regulation.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 2.4:</term> Construct and interpret models of aquifers, demonstrating how groundwater is stored, recharged, and accessed, and how aquifer characteristics vary across regions.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 2.5:</term> Analyze the ecological and societal implications of freshwater distribution, including disparities in water access, consumption patterns, and the impact of human activities on water availability and quality.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 2.6:</term> Evaluate the role of oceans in supporting life and regulating climate, with emphasis on upwelling zones, thermohaline circulation, and the ecological importance of marine systems.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 2.7:</term> Assess the impact of climate change on freshwater reservoirs, particularly glacial melt and its contribution to sea level rise and aquifer recharge.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>LO 2.8:</term> Identify threats to freshwater systems, including pollution, overconsumption, impermeable surfaces, and drought, and propose sustainable management strategies.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>
    
